---
import SectionLayout from '../layouts/SectionLayout.astro';
import PlainEnglish from '../components/PlainEnglish.astro';
import BarChart from '../components/BarChart.astro';
import { withBase } from '../utils/url';

const marketShare = [
  { label: 'NVIDIA', value: 80, display: '~75–85%', color: 'accent' as const },
  { label: 'AMD', value: 8.5, display: '~7–10%', color: 'teal' as const },
  { label: 'Custom silicon', value: 11.5, display: 'most of the rest', sub: 'Google, AWS, Microsoft, Meta', color: 'highlight' as const },
];

const memoryCapacity = [
  { label: 'H100', value: 80, display: '80 GB', sub: 'HBM3', color: 'accent' as const },
  { label: 'H200', value: 141, display: '141 GB', sub: 'HBM3e', color: 'accent' as const },
  { label: 'MI300X', value: 192, display: '192 GB+', sub: 'HBM3', color: 'teal' as const },
  { label: 'B200', value: 192, display: '192 GB', sub: 'HBM3e', color: 'highlight' as const },
];
---

<SectionLayout
  slug="gpu-models"
  kicker="Section 02"
  title="GPU models"
  dek="What GPUs are, why they run AI, and how the NVIDIA lineup, AMD, and custom silicon compare."
>
  <PlainEnglish>
    <p>
      A GPU is the chip that does the actual "thinking" for AI. Different GPUs are like
      different-sized brains — some are cheaper and good enough for most jobs (H100), some have a
      bigger memory so they can hold a bigger model in their head at once (H200, B200), and some
      companies build their own custom chips instead of buying from NVIDIA to save money (Google's
      TPU, Amazon's Trainium). Teaching a model (training) and using it to answer a question
      (inference) are different jobs that favor different chips.
    </p>
  </PlainEnglish>

  <h2>Why GPUs at all</h2>
  <p>
    GPUs were originally built to render graphics — pushing the same simple calculation across millions of pixels
    at once. That "do the same math on huge amounts of data in parallel" design turns out to be exactly what
    training and running AI models needs, since both are fundamentally massive matrix multiplication. That's the
    whole reason GPUs, not traditional CPUs, became the default hardware for AI.
  </p>

  <h2>Training vs. inference: different jobs, different hardware priorities</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Training</strong> — teaching a model by processing enormous datasets and adjusting its parameters. Extremely compute- and memory-bandwidth-heavy; benefits most from raw FLOPS and fast chip-to-chip interconnect across huge clusters.</li>
    <li><strong>Inference</strong> — running an already-trained model to answer a single prompt. Less compute per request, but memory capacity matters a lot (bigger memory means a whole model, or more of it, fits on fewer chips) and it happens at massive volume, so cost-per-token becomes the deciding metric.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    This split explains most of the hardware choices below: newer/bigger-memory chips are increasingly being
    reserved for high-value inference, while training runs are often done on a cost-efficient mix of current and
    older generations.
  </p>

  <div class="callout-note">
    <strong>Why B200-class chips need liquid cooling:</strong> Blackwell draws roughly 1,000W per GPU — several
    times a traditional server chip — so the air-cooling used in most existing data center halls can't keep it
    within temperature limits. That's one reason "AI / GPU hall" is becoming its own <a href={withBase('/structural-stack')}>data center category</a>: purpose-built, liquid-cooled, and denser than a standard facility.
  </div>

  <h2>The NVIDIA lineup <span class="section-note">(roughly 75–85% of the market)</span></h2>
  <div class="table-scroll">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Chip</th><th>Memory</th><th>Notable trait</th><th>Best fit</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>H100</td>
          <td>80 GB HBM3</td>
          <td>The default workhorse since 2023; cheapest and most available of the current generation</td>
          <td>Cost-sensitive training, general-purpose workloads</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>H200</td>
          <td>141 GB HBM3e, ~4.8 TB/s bandwidth</td>
          <td>Same core chip as H100, just a big memory upgrade — same compute, far more memory</td>
          <td>Serving long-context models and models up to ~100B params without needing multiple GPUs</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>B200 (Blackwell)</td>
          <td>192 GB HBM3e, native FP4 support</td>
          <td>A genuinely new architecture: ~4x H100's inference throughput, but 1,000W and requires liquid cooling</td>
          <td>High-value production inference, new large training runs where the throughput gain pays for the higher cost</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>GB200 / GB200 NVL72</td>
          <td>72 B200 GPUs + 36 Grace CPUs per rack</td>
          <td>A full rack wired together with NVLink to behave like one giant GPU</td>
          <td>Frontier-scale training and inference at the data-center level</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    NVIDIA's next generation, <strong>Vera Rubin</strong>, is expected in the second half of 2026 with even more
    memory and bandwidth — the pattern of "new generation roughly every year, each one a step-change in memory
    and throughput" is likely to continue.
  </p>

  <h2>The AMD alternative</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>MI300X / MI325X</strong> — AMD's answer to NVIDIA, competing mainly on price and memory: comparable
    throughput at meaningfully lower cost per chip, with very large memory pools (192 GB+) that let a single chip
    serve a full 70-billion-parameter model without splitting it across multiple GPUs. The tradeoff is NVIDIA's
    much more mature software ecosystem (CUDA), so switching to AMD (ROCm) takes real engineering effort. AMD
    holds roughly 7–10% of the market.
  </p>

  <h2>Custom silicon — hyperscalers building their own chips</h2>
  <p>
    The biggest cloud companies are increasingly designing their own AI chips instead of only buying NVIDIA's,
    mainly to cut cost and reduce dependency:
  </p>
  <div class="table-scroll">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Chip</th><th>Built by</th><th>Focus</th><th>Notable detail</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>TPU (v6 "Trillium", v7 "Ironwood")</td>
          <td>Google</td>
          <td>Training and inference</td>
          <td>Runs Google's own models (Gemini) and is now also used by outside customers — Anthropic and Meta have both signed large TPU commitments</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Trainium</td>
          <td>AWS</td>
          <td>Training and inference</td>
          <td>Only available through AWS — signing up for Trainium means signing up for AWS as your sole provider</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Maia</td>
          <td>Microsoft</td>
          <td>Inference-focused</td>
          <td>Powers Copilot and OpenAI API traffic on Azure</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>MTIA</td>
          <td>Meta</td>
          <td>Inference-focused</td>
          <td>Built for Meta's own internal workloads (recommendation systems, some AI inference)</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    Custom silicon can cut inference costs 30–50% versus equivalent GPU capacity, since the chip, network, and
    software framework are all designed together — but it locks a customer into that one cloud provider's
    ecosystem.
  </p>

  <h2>The market snapshot</h2>
  <p>Roughly how AI accelerator revenue splits today — though custom ASICs are growing faster in unit terms than GPU-based systems:</p>
  <BarChart items={marketShare} max={100} />

  <h3>Memory capacity, side by side</h3>
  <p>The single spec that most directly explains why certain chips fit certain models — more memory means a bigger model fits on fewer chips:</p>
  <BarChart items={memoryCapacity} />
</SectionLayout>

<style>
  .section-note {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.55em;
    font-weight: 500;
    color: var(--text-tertiary);
    text-transform: none;
    letter-spacing: 0;
  }
</style>
